96: ## 3. Decisions — the mechanism under the ruled design
97: 
98: - **D1. Pool root.** `<LOCALAPPDATA ?? XDG_CACHE_HOME ?? tmpdir>/ai4good-build/db-slots/`,
99:   the same base-resolution rule as the runner's `lockDir()` (F3), so the pool lives beside the
100:   locks it cooperates with, outside every worktree. Layout: `slot-1/supabase/…`,
101:   `slot-2/supabase/…`, `reservations/slot-N.json`. Env override `AT_DB_POOL_ROOT` exists for
102:   the selftests only, exactly as `AT_REPO_ROOT` exists for the runner's own tests.
103: - **D2. Slot identity.** Slot N owns `project_id "ai4good-slot-N"` and the port overlay
104:   `54xxx + N*1000`: slot-1 = 55320/55321/55322/55323/55324/55327/55329, inspector 8093;
105:   slot-2 = 56320/56321/56322/56323/56324/56327/56329, inspector 8103. Distinct project ids
106:   give distinct Docker container and volume names; distinct port blocks give the wall the
107:   spike proves. No slot value may fall in 54320–54329 or be 8083 (D5 enforces).
108:   The overlay rule is GENERAL (gate-1 [5]): every active key named `port` or ending `_port`,
109:   in every section, with a value in 54000–54999, maps to value + N*1000.
110:   `edge_runtime.inspector_port` maps 8083 → 8083 + N*10. Any other active port value refuses
111:   loudly as unmappable — a human decides, not a guess.
112: - **D3. Slot config regeneration — identity is permanent, everything else is data.** The ruled
113:   identity/data split names project id + ports as the slot's identity. Auth flags, seed paths
114:   and `verify_jwt` pins also live in `config.toml`, and items change them (F4's
115:   `enable_confirmations` was flipped by an item). A slot config frozen at setup time would
116:   drift from the item tree's config, and a verify on a slot would grade different auth
117:   behavior than a verify on the stack the config describes. So `prepare()` regenerates the
118:   slot's `config.toml` on every occupancy: take the item tree's `supabase/config.toml`
119:   verbatim, overlay ONLY the identity fields (project_id, the seven port keys, inspector_port)
120:   with the slot's permanent values, using the same section-aware line scan `readLocalConfig`
121:   already uses, and the general port rule in D2. The keep-warm decision compares against a
122:   MARKER, not the file (gate-1 [1]): `slot-N/.last-start.json` holds the hash of the config the
123:   stack last STARTED with, written only after a successful start. Regenerated hash equals the
124:   marker → nothing happens, the warm stack keeps running (the ruled default). Different or
125:   absent marker → prepare writes the config, restarts that slot's stack (`stop` + `start
126:   --workdir`) before the reset — the auth container reads config at start, not at reset — and
127:   writes the marker only when the restart succeeds. A crash between the write and the restart
128:   therefore forces a restart on the next occupancy instead of hiding behind equal text.
129: - **D4. Occupancy = the existing stack lock, extended.** `occupy()` acquires
130:   `acquireStackLock(slotConfig, requirement)` (F3) — it is already the atomic create-new,
131:   pid-stamped, stale-takeover-with-loud-line claim the ruled text describes, and it is already
132:   released in the runner's `finally` chain. Two extensions. First: when a stale claim is
133:   broken, the new claim file records `tookOverFrom` (the dead holder's pid and start time), so
134:   the takeover is recorded IN the claim, not only on the console. Second (gate-1 [3]):
135:   `acquireStackLock` gains a takeover-policy parameter; the pool passes dead-pid-only, so a
136:   LIVE holder is never taken over at any age — occupy refuses loudly and names the holder.
137:   Existing call sites keep today's default policy unchanged.
138: - **D5. The personal block is refused in code, not by convention.** db-pool refuses to occupy,
139:   prepare, reset or emit env for any slot whose config carries the repo config's `project_id`,
140:   any port in 54320–54329, or inspector port 8083. The scan covers EVERY active port-valued
141:   key in the config, not a fixed list (gate-1 [5]). The check runs before anything
142:   destructive, every time, and has its own named selftest. This is the "untouchable" ruling as
143:   an executable guard.
144: - **D6. Reservation lookup is derived, never declared.** The coordinator's `Reserve-DbSlot`
145:   writes `reservations/slot-N.json` (exclusive create; content: item id, branch, timestamp,
146:   holder). At verify time the runner derives its item id from the current branch (the house
147:   attribution rule) and occupies the slot whose reservation names that item. A run with no
148:   reservation for its item refuses as INFRASTRUCTURE, naming `Reserve-DbSlot` — no silent
149:   fallback onto any free slot, because admission control is the coordinator's, not the
150:   runner's. `AT_DB_SLOT=N` is the explicit override for runs outside an item (the founder, the
151:   evidence gate, the spike); the occupancy claim still applies.
152:   Three hardenings (gate-1 [2], [12]): the branch parser is a pure function over the branch
153:   string and FAILS CLOSED — exactly one item id, or it refuses naming the condition (zero ids,
154:   several ids, detached HEAD, git call failed); `occupy` re-reads the reservation AFTER the
155:   claim is acquired and releases-then-refuses if it no longer names the runner's item;
156:   `Release-DbSlot` deletes a reservation only when it names the item being swept, and refuses
157:   loudly while a live occupancy claim exists on that slot.
158: - **D7. Data copy at occupancy.** Before the reset, prepare deletes and re-copies into the
159:   slot: `supabase/migrations/*.sql` (timestamped files only, the runner's own pattern),
160:   `supabase/seed.sql` when the item tree has one — and removes the slot's copy when it does
161:   not (F4) — and `supabase/functions/**` (F5: integration suites can call edge functions
162:   through the injected URL). The previous holder's files are never trusted; delete-then-copy,
163:   not merge.
164:   Fail closed on the closure (gate-1 [6]): after regeneration, prepare scans the config for
165:   active relative-path values (seed `sql_paths`, `schema_paths`, email template files, TLS and
166:   signing key paths) and refuses loudly on any path it cannot copy from the item tree — no
167:   stack starts half-provisioned. Env policy, decided: slot start and restart inherit the
168:   invoking process env; a missed `env()` resolves to a literal string and does not stop the
169:   stack (F6, measured). [The inherit-env sentence is SUPERSEDED by ruling E1's strip — see §9,
170:   flash note 1: every `env()` substitution resolves to the literal string on a slot, always.]
171: - **D8. The runner hook is integration-tier only.** In main's stack block: when
172:   `tier === 'integration'`, resolve the stack through the pool — occupy → prepare (copy,
173:   config, reset via `--workdir`, readiness, prove migrations) → the same prove-local checks
174:   against the SLOT's config → inject the slot's env — and print the evidence line. The loop
175:   tier stays byte-identical (it never enters the block). The DRILL tier REFUSES as
176:   INFRASTRUCTURE (gate-1 [4]): "the drill tier's stack is not yet decided" — it no longer
177:   reaches any stack, personal or slot. Nothing in the tree invokes `--tier drill` today
178:   (verified: no script, no CI step, no process file), so nothing breaks, and the last harness
179:   path that could reset the personal stack is closed in code. The follow-up item that decides
180:   drill's stack replaces the refusal.
181: - **D9. Existing helpers are parameterized, not duplicated.** `readLocalConfig`,
182:   `readStackStatus`, `resetLocalDatabase` and `proveMigrationsReplayed` currently hard-code
183:   REPO_ROOT (F1). Each gains a workdir/config parameter defaulting to today's value —
184:   loop-tier call-site behavior is unchanged — and the pool path passes the slot.
185:   No second copy of the status parser, the prove-local checks or the reset process handling.
186:   `lockDir()` honors an `AT_LOCK_DIR` env override (gate-1 [13]), the same pattern as
187:   `AT_REPO_ROOT`; the default is unchanged; the db-pool selftests set it so their claims never
188:   touch the machine-wide lock directory.
189: - **D10. One place builds configs.** All TOML reading and generation lives in db-pool.ts
190:   (TypeScript). The one-time setup is a CLI entry in the same file (`bun
191:   tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts setup`, the `import.meta.main` pattern runner.ts uses): create
192:   both slot directories, generate both configs from the current tree + identity overlay, start
193:   both stacks via the pinned CLI, print both status reports. The PowerShell helpers
194:   (`loop/work/db-slots.ps1`: `Reserve-DbSlot`, `Release-DbSlot`, `Get-DbSlotPool`) do file
195:   operations only — reserve, release, list — mirroring the `Acquire-WorkLock` house pattern
196:   (F9). No TOML logic in PowerShell, so the overlay cannot fork.
197: - **D11. The evidence line.** One line on the verify transcript, printed by the runner on the
198:   pool path: `at:verify — db slot <N> (<project_id>, api <port>) — reset OK — migrations: <E>
199:   expected, <A> applied`. Slot, reset performed, migration state — the ruled three.
200: - **D12. No slicing.** The diff is one mechanism — pool module, its selftests, one runner
201:   hook, small PowerShell helpers, and process-file lines. The parts reference each other; a
202:   per-slice code gate would review the claim logic apart from the tests that pin it. One
203:   draft-code gate reads the whole diff.
204: - **D13. One shared invocation helper (ruling E1, §8; added by the resumed draft sitting,
205:   2026-08-10).** Every slot CLI invocation goes through one helper in db-pool.ts. The helper:
206:   sets `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=<slot project id>` positively in the child env; strips every other
207:   `SUPABASE_*` variable; invokes `bun --no-env-file` per the house rule; sets the child working
208:   directory to the slot. The destructive path (reset, stop) performs an identity read through
209:   the same helper first — the status the CLI reports must carry the slot's ports, and any
210:   project identity it reports must be the slot's — and refuses loudly on mismatch. The read is
211:   structurally ON the destructive path, never a separate call a caller can skip. This applies
212:   to every slot-aimed use of the parameterized runner helpers (D9) too.
