643: ## 9. Gate-2 rulings (fix sitting, orchestrator on fable, 2026-08-10)
644: 
645: Gate 2 was a panel of two, each blind to the other. Reader one: terra via codex @ max,
646: read-only sandbox — 13 findings, distillate `artifacts/gate2-terra-distillate.md`, count line
647: matched. Reader two: flash via opencode, agent `reviewer-flash`, variant max — 9 findings plus
648: four notes outside the findings, distillate `artifacts/gate2-flash-distillate.md`, count line
649: matched. Every finding is ruled here with the claim quoted verbatim. Neither seat outvotes the
650: other; convergences are named because a panel converging on one defect is its strongest signal.
651: 
652: Dispositions: terra — 8 accepted, 4 accepted-fixed-differently, 1 rejected. flash — 9 accepted,
653: 0 rejected; all four notes ruled below. No ruling removes work, so no removal verification
654: conditions exist. One ruling ([T2]) carries a verify-first component for the executor.
655: 
656: ### Terra's findings
657: 
658: **[T1] ACCEPT — the critical.** Claim: "An empty or partially written lock file is treated as a
659: dead holder under `dead-pid-only`, so a second occupier can delete a live process's just-created
660: claim."
661: Verified at runner.ts:440: `readHolder` returns `{}` for an unreadable or half-written file, and
662: `holderIsLive({})` is false under every policy — so an unidentifiable holder is takeover-eligible
663: even under the policy whose ruled meaning is "a DEAD HOLDER PID and nothing else". The window is
664: the microseconds between `openSync(file, 'wx')` and the `writeSync`, and it is real. Fix, scoped
665: to `dead-pid-only`: an unidentifiable holder is NEVER takeover-eligible. After one bounded
666: re-read (a short pause, then read again — enough to skate over the write window), a holder that
667: still has no parseable pid refuses loudly, naming the file and the manual deletion path. The
668: same guard applies to the re-read INSIDE the takeover gate, so the gate cannot remove an
669: unidentifiable file either. `stale-or-dead` keeps today's behaviour: that path predates this
670: item and is another item's business if anyone wants it changed.
671: 
672: **[T2] ACCEPT, with a verify-first component.** Claim: "`proveSlotTarget` accepts a valid status
673: result containing zero `supabase_*` container tokens, so its project-identity check can pass
674: vacuously."
675: True by code: `foreignContainerNames` on empty input returns nothing, `carriesPersonal` is
676: false, and the read proceeds on port checks alone — the exact hybrid shape the incident wore
677: (right ports, wrong project) passes if the CLI prints no container names. Fail-closed (E7) says
678: absence of identity evidence must refuse a destructive act. VERIFY FIRST (executor, read-only):
679: run `status -o json` through the helper on a slot and measure whether `supabase_*` tokens appear
680: in the combined output. Then: destructive acts require POSITIVE identity evidence — at least one
681: `supabase_*` token ending in the slot's project id when the measurement shows tokens appear; and
682: in every case a docker READ (`docker ps` filtered on the slot's own project id) must confirm the
683: slot's own db container exists before a reset. Docker reads are the one permitted instrument on
684: the untouchable stack and are unrestricted on slots. This also narrows [F4]'s residual: the CLI
685: output shape is no longer the only instrument.
686: 
687: **[T3] ACCEPT — amends ruling E3 (convergence with flash [F3]).** Claim: "The full-tree mirror
688: copies ignored `supabase/.temp` and `.branches` runtime state into slots."
689: Both seats found this independently. `supabase/.temp` is the CLI's runtime state about the
690: PERSONAL stack — and `.gitignore` itself says its `start-secrets/**` "must NEVER be committed" —
691: so the mirror carries another stack's identity residue and secrets into every slot. E3's
692: "mirror entire, no filter" is narrowed, not removed: the mirror carries the item tree's PROJECT
693: SOURCE entire; the CLI's own runtime directories `.temp` and `.branches` are not project source
694: and are excluded, as is `config.toml` (see [T11]). `pathClosureProblems` gains the matching
695: refusal: a config path that resolves into `.temp` or `.branches` refuses loudly, so the closure
696: guarantee stays honest about the exclusions.
697: 
698: **[T4] ACCEPT (convergence with flash note 2).** Claim: "The `spike` command resets slot 2
699: without acquiring either slot's occupancy claim."
700: D6's own ruled sentence — "the occupancy claim still applies" for override runs — is not
701: implemented by the spike, which calls `resetSlotDatabase` and writes canaries with no claim
702: held. Fix: the spike occupies both slots through the normal `occupy` path (dead-pid-only
703: policy) before touching either, and releases both in a `finally`.
704: 
705: **[T5] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Claim: "`AT_DB_SLOT` bypasses branch-derived reservation
706: ownership and the post-claim reservation reread even when invoked from a normal item branch."
707: The framing over-claims: slot STATE is disposable by design ("state is never inherited" — every
708: occupancy resets from its own tree), so the wall against destruction is the occupancy claim,
709: which the override keeps, not the reservation, which is admission control. The override itself
710: is ruled (D6: the founder, the evidence gate, the spike). But a silent stomp on a slot another
711: item holds is real and cheap to refuse: `occupy` via override now refuses loudly when the
712: target slot carries a reservation naming a DIFFERENT item than this run can derive (from
713: `options.item` or the branch); a run that can derive no item treats ANY reservation as foreign.
714: The refusal names the reservation holder. An unreserved slot, or one reserved for this run's
715: own item, proceeds as today.
716: 
717: **[T6] ACCEPT.** Claim: "Occupancy locks the previous slot config, while `prepare` can rewrite
718: that config's listener ports from the current item tree."
719: The claim file's name derives from `projectId + apiPort` read from the slot's CURRENT on-disk
720: config, which `prepare` may lawfully rewrite — so two runs bracketing a port change would hold
721: two different lock files for one slot. Fix: slot claims are keyed on the slot's PERMANENT
722: identity — project id `ai4good-slot-N` plus a fixed sentinel port — used by `occupy` and by
723: `readPool`'s occupancy read, and documented at the key. The PowerShell occupancy glob
724: (`at-verify-ai4good-slot-N-*.lock`) already matches any suffix and is unchanged.
725: 
726: **[T7] ACCEPT (convergence with flash [F1] — same line, both seats).** Claim:
727: "`resetSlotDatabase`, `stopSlotStack`, and `stackEnv` do not run the broad D5 personal-block
728: guard themselves."
729: D5's ruled sentence names "occupy, prepare, reset or emit env"; the code runs `refusePersonal`
730: on the first two only, and the last two are exported entry points a caller can reach directly —
731: the spike does. Fix: each of the three runs `refusePersonal` over the slot's on-disk config
732: text before acting. `stackEnv` keeps its inline URL-port checks besides — they check the
733: STATUS, which the config guard cannot see.
734: 
735: **[T8] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY — one half of the claim is wrong and is recorded as such.**
736: Claim: "`edge_runtime.inspector_port` is remapped from any value, including non-numeric values,
737: instead of refusing every value other than the ruled 8083."
738: The non-numeric half is false: the `!literal` branch at db-pool.ts:242 refuses before the
739: inspector case is reached. The numeric half is right: any numeric inspector value is remapped
740: by +N*10, which is a guess for every value except the ruled 8083, and a guess is what D2
741: forbids. Fix: the special case pins to exactly 8083; any other inspector value falls through to
742: the generic listener rule — in-band maps, out-of-band refuses loudly.
743: 
744: **[T9] REJECT.** Claim: "`Release-DbSlot` checks occupancy and deletes the reservation without
745: an atomic handoff."
746: The TOCTOU window is real and changes nothing destructive. The serializer for destructive acts
747: is the occupancy claim (dead-pid-only; a live holder is never displaced): a runner that slips
748: into the window still holds its claim, so the worst outcome is the NEXT item's occupy refusing
749: loudly until the window closes — a loud refusal, not a reset under a live run. An atomic
750: two-file handoff in PowerShell 5.1 would buy no safety the claim does not already provide.
751: Recorded as a residual beside the sweep helper's comment.
752: 
753: **[T10] ACCEPT.** Claim: "Two concurrent `Reserve-DbSlot` calls for the same item can reserve
754: both slots."
755: True by walk-through: both pass the already-held scan, one wins slot 1, the loser's catch
756: records "held" and advances to slot 2, and one item holds the whole pool. Fix: in the
757: create-failure catch, re-read the reservation; when it names the same item, return
758: `alreadyHeld` for that slot instead of advancing.
759: 
760: **[T11] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Claim: "An interruption between delete/copy and
761: regenerated-config write can permanently strand a slot before the marker recovery logic runs."
762: Ruling this surfaced something worse than stranding: between the mirror and the config write,
763: the slot's `config.toml` IS the item tree's config — the personal identity — on disk in a slot
764: directory. A crash in that window leaves a slot wearing the personal identity (occupy would
765: refuse loudly, but the file should never exist at all). Fix: the mirror excludes `config.toml`
766: (with [T3]'s exclusions, one mechanism) and the generated config is written immediately after —
767: so no slot config ever carries the personal identity, even transiently, and a crash in the
768: window leaves NO config rather than a wrong one. The missing-config refusal in `occupy` names
769: both causes (never set up; a prepare that died mid-window) and the one-command repair. Not
770: permanent, and loud at every step.
771: 
772: **[T12] ACCEPT (convergence with flash [F2], second half).** Claim: "The personal Docker
773: snapshot can report `IDENTICAL` when both before/after queries return zero containers and
774: volumes."
775: A vacuous pass on the spike's central instrument — gate 1's whole theme. Fix: a new spike
776: criterion requires the BEFORE snapshot non-empty (at least one personal container AND one
777: personal volume recorded); an empty snapshot fails the run.
778: 
779: **[T13] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Claim: "The path-closure check validates lexical paths but
780: does not resolve symlinks under `supabase/`."
781: Real, and resolving symlinks lexically is the wrong instrument. Fix, fail-closed and total: the
782: mirror refuses loudly on ANY symlink found under the item tree's `supabase/` before copying.
783: The tree carries none today (the executor confirms the scan passes), so nothing breaks, and a
784: symlink smuggled in later refuses instead of copying as a link that points wherever it likes.
