oof)\n90: \n91: ================================================================================\n92: POSTSCRIPT, written by hand after the run.\n93: ================================================================================\n94: \n95: THE TREE THE RUN USED. The head line above says 3ca44eb, which was the last\n96: commit at the moment of the run. The working tree also carried one uncommitted\n97: edit: writeCanary drops and recreates its table instead of using CREATE TABLE IF\n98: NOT EXISTS. Both are in the commit that carries this transcript.\n99: \n100: THE FIRST ATTEMPT, recorded because a transcript that hides one is not evidence.\n101: The spike was run once before this run and stopped at step (c) with:\n102: \n103:   null value in column \"id\" of relation \"spike_canary\" violates not-null constraint\n104: \n105: Slot 1 still held a spike_canary table left by the first, failed spike of\n106: 2026-08-09, with a column set of its own. CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS therefore\n107: created nothing and the insert failed on that table's constraint. The stop was a\n108: canary client problem in a slot database. No identity was resolved, no CLI\n109: command ran after the preflight reads, and step (h) of that attempt already\n110: reported the personal docker snapshot IDENTICAL. The fix was to drop and\n111: recreate the canary table, and the run above is the re-run.\n112: \n113: TWO INDEPENDENT READS AGREE THAT THE PERSONAL STACK WAS NOT TOUCHED. Step (h)\n114: compares the docker identity snapshot and reports it identical. A separate\n115: docker ps taken after the run reports every personal container still up with the\n116: same uptime it had before the run, so nothing recreated or restarted one. The\n117: tracked .env is byte for byte unchanged, verified by SHA-256 before and after.\n118: \n119: END OF POSTSCRIPT\n\n(End of file - total 119 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\nAI4DEV-79 (parallel DB slot pool) - step S3 as amended by ruling E5, the i